WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.650
The idea of an actor fluffing up their resume
with unreal skills might sound like something

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.580
out of a sitcom, but plenty of actors have
been caught red-handed.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.000
Here's a fond look back at a few of those
major actors who've lied to get the big part.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.520
It's hard to believe that a hunk like Chris
Hemsworth, who has flexed both his massive

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.670
muscles and his funny bone as the Asgardian
God of Thunder Thor in the Marvel Cinematic

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.550
Universe, would ever have to lie in order
to persuade casting directors to give him

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.040
a role.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.760
Just as Hemsworth surprised us with his comedic
timing in Thor: Ragnarok …

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:29.460
"Hang on.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:30.560
Give it a second.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:32.980
I swear I'm not even moving.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.000
It's just doing this on its own."

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:38.520
... he shocked fans when he admitted to Radio
Times that he used to lie about his height,

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.020
of all things, to get more work.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.760
The 6-foot-3 Aussie admitted,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.700
"There are certainly things I've wanted to
go up for which I've been totally wrong for,

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:46.710
physically.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.880
And I normally lie about my height and say
I'm shorter.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.440
But it can go two ways.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.300
The brief for the audition for Thor said:
must be over 6ft 1in, which I'd never seen

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:55.800
before!"

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:00.780
To score the role of Queen drummer Roger Taylor
in the Bryan Singer-directed biopic Bohemian

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.860
Rhapsody, Ben Hardy got caught up in his own
little fantasy.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.380
Hardy really wanted the job, and told Singer
what he called "a big lie" about his drumming

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.620
skills in hopes of getting hired for the film.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.030
Having previously collaborated with Singer
for X-Men: Apocalypse, Hardy sent the director

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:20.000
an email inquiring about Bohemian Rhapsody
and asking, quote, "in the nicest way possible"

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:21.800
if Singer thought he could be in the film.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.800
Hardy told People,

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.440
"I told [Singer] I could play the drums, which
I couldn't at the time."

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:30.820
Singer asked Hardy to record himself drumming
to a song to show the producers, and Hardy

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.560
panicked, but then he got to work.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:33.560
"Ready Freddy?"

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:34.560
"Let's do it."

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.780
"I bought the cheapest drum kit I could find
and found a drum teacher locally and was like,

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.680
'Look, here's the deal I need to learn to
play this song as soon as possible, what can

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:43.180
you do?'"

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.700
Luckily for Hardy, his lie, and his training,
paid off big time.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.660
It's hardly a secret that Robert Pattinson
has doctored his resume in the past, falsely

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:55.950
claiming that he attended Oxford University
and London's illustrious drama school the

00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:00.180
Royal Academy of Dramatic Arts as a way to
score roles after moving to America.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.000
But alongside that deceit, he also lied about
his nationality in order to make himself more

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:07.110
marketable to Hollywood big shots.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.040
As Pattinson confessed to the Daily Mirror,

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.480
"I [lied about my education] for years.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.280
But it didn't work when loads of English people
started moving to Los Angeles.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.420
So then I pretended to be American for a bit."

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.900
Desperate times call for desperate measures,
and in the case of character actor Todd Lawson

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.460
LaTourrette, a difficult situation drove him
to do the unthinkable.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.120
An amputee and self-described war veteran,
LaTourrette built a satisfactory career in

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.350
film and on television, using his missing
hand and the trauma of his past to inform

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:36.460
his approach to roles.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.080
"How exactly did you lose it?

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:40.640
Your arm?"

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.780
"I was riding shotgun in a Humvee in Kandahar.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:45.840
We drove right over an IED."

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.720
Casting directors liked what they saw and
tapped him for parts in films like The Men

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.060
Who Stare at Goats, Beer for My Horses, and
on television series including Longmire, and

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.319
the acclaimed Breaking Bad spin-off Better
Call Saul, where he made a blink-and-you'll-miss-it

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:58.840
appearance.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.380
But LaTourrette's career was founded on a
lie.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.720
In November 2018, he revealed that he'd been
dishonest about being a war veteran and that

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.880
he didn't lose his hand while serving overseas.

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:14.640
The truth of the matter is arguably even grimmer:
17 years before, LaTourette, experiencing

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:18.830
what he described as a "psychotic episode"
during a time he was off medication meant

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:22.560
to manage his bipolar disorder, severed his
own hand with a saw.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.300
His lie worked, but the guilt was too much
to bear, so LaTourette decided to come clean.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:28.520
"I was dishonorable.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.980
I'm killing my career by doing this."

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:37.880
"If anybody wants to think this was for personal
edification, that's not the case."

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.600
Laurence Fishburne has done it all.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.860
From scoring an Academy Award nomination for
his performance as Ike Turner in What's Love

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:50.150
Got to Do with It to dipping his toes into
the DC Extended Universe and the MCU, Fishburne

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:51.380
is something of a legend.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:53.360
"WOOOOWEEEEE!"

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.700
But he's also a little bit of a liar, too,
as the actor fibbed to director Francis Ford

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.120
Coppola so he could star in Apocalypse Now.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:06.180
In 1976, a then-14-year-old Fishburne told
Coppola that he was actually 16 in efforts

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.860
to boost his chances at landing the role of
17-year-old Tyrone "Mr. Clean" Miller.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.750
Coppola bought the lie and cast Fishburne
in the war epic, which was famously fraught

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:16.740
with production issues.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:22.559
By the time the film finally debuted in theaters
in May of 1979, Fishburne was actually as

00:04:22.559 --> 00:04:23.620
old as his character.

